(11. Oops, Should?ve Thought of That) Tj
T*
(Source: Jim Nielsen?s Blog | Published: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 19:00:00 GMT) Tj
T*
(Link: https://blog.jim-nielsen.com/2026/oops-shouldve-thought-of-that/) Tj
T*
(Gordon Brander writes : The thing about computers is that they can do anything you wish, so) Tj
T*
(long as you specify your wish in exacting detail. However, LLMs relieve this constraint. An LLM) Tj
T*
(can extrapolate what you mean \(more or less\) from just a few words. So computers can do vibes) Tj
T*
(now. It used to be that computers could only ever do exactly what you told them to do, nothing) Tj
T*
(more. If they did something unexpected \(a.k.a. a bug\), it was because you failed to 1\)) Tj
T*
(anticipate it could happen, or 2\) specify with greater detail that it shouldn?t happen. But in) Tj
T*
(this new world, computers can extrapolate your intent. LLMS can act like a fill-in-the-gap) Tj
T*
(machine, which alleviates you from the pedantic demands of coding from years past . There?s) Tj
T*
(going to be a lot more ?Oops, I didn?t mean to do that? in the future. This should be what) Tj
T*
(separates professional practitioners from hobbyists. A good practitioner recognizes that) Tj
T*
(leveraging some foresight is part and parcel of the job in order to mitigate unintended) Tj
T*
(consequences. Meanwhile, a hobbyist or amateur doesn?t even have the capability or experience) Tj
T*
(to understand the assumed intent of an LLM until it?s too late and users have been affected.) Tj
T*
(So...) Tj
